ybe threatening something?  \n\nWhite’s diagonal from D7(O), E8(O), F8? no F8 X, so not threat. But White has open 3? Possibly from H9(O), G10(O), F11(O), E12(O) — that’s 4 in a row diagonal (H9–E12 all O). That’s **open 4 for White**, lethal threat! White can win next turn by playing either C13 (northwest end) or D13 (southeast extension?) Let’s trace:  \nDiagonal (southwest to northeast):  \nD7(O), E8(O), F9 empty, G10(O), H9(O), I8(O) no I8 X, so that diagonal not 4 O’s.  \n\nActually the diagonal from E12 to H9: E12(O), F11(O), G10(O), H9(O). That’s 4 O’s in a row diagonally (direction: northwest–southeast?). Wait coordinates: E12, F11, G10, H9: each step is (+1 col, -1 row) so that’s diagonal going up-right.  \nExtensions: up-right from H9: I8 is X (blocked), down-left from E12: D13 empty. So that 4 is **open** on D13 side, but blocked on I8 side? But in gomoku, open 4 means both ends open, this is only one end open (D13), so it’s a “broken 4” still threatening 5 if D13 played.  \n\nLet’s check if it’s really 4 O’s consecutive in that diagonal: Yes E12(O), F11(O), G10(O), H9(O) — consecutive in that diagonal. Next square toward H9’s upward-right is I8 (X) — blocked. Next square toward E12’s downward-left is D13 (empty). So White can win by playing D13 next turn (making O at D13, C14, etc. — actually E12–D13–C14–B15 would be more than 5 but anyway D13 completes 5 O’s: H9, G10, F11, E12, D13).  \n\nThus **I must block at D13** to stop White from winning next move.  \n\nIs D13 occupied? No (from occupied list not there).  \nIs it valid? Yes.  \nSo my move: D13.\n\n**Reason:**  \nWhite has a diagonal four (H9–G10–F11–E12) with an open end at D13. If I don’t block D13, White wins next turn. Therefore, blocking is forced. No immediate win for me in one move, so defense is top priority.\n\n**Answer:**  \n```json\n{"col": "D", "row": 13}\n```'}
