So possible unbroken four-in-a-row for Black?  \nCheck:  \nA8–D8: X X X X (four).  \nF8–I8: X X X X (four).  \nK8–M8: X O X — no.  \nM8–O8: X X X (three only).  \n\nActually, check M8–O8: M8 X, N8 X, O8 X → that’s three X’s, not four.  \n\nSo the only four-in-a-row Black has are A8–D8 and F8–I8. Both are blocked at both ends.  \n\nTherefore, no immediate five-in-a-row possible in row 8.  \n\nWe must check **vertical and diagonal** four-in-a-rows.  \n\nVertically:  \nColumn D: D7 O, D8 X, D9 ., D10 ., D11 ., D12 ., D13 X — not four.  \nColumn G: G7 X, G8 X, G9 ., G10 O, G11 ., G12 O — not four.  \nColumn H: H4 O, H6 O, H8 X, H9 X — not four.  \n\nNo vertical four.  \n\nDiagonally:  \nCheck from A8 down-right: A8 X, B9 O — no.  \nCheck from D7 down-right: D7 O, E8 O, F9 X, G10 O — no.  \nCheck from G7 down-right: G7 X, H8 X, I9 X — three, not four.  \n\nNo diagonal four.  \n\nSo no immediate winning move by completing five in a row.  \n\nThen we must **create a threat** like an open three or four that forces opponent into defense while setting up future win.  \n\nThe most promising area is **near the dense region** row 8–9, column G–I.  \n\nLook at G9 (empty).  \nIf Black plays G9:  \n- Horizontal: F8 X, G8 X, H8 X, I8 X, but J8 O — so from G9 leftwards: G9 X, F8 X, E8 O — no five. But wait, G9 connects G8 and H8, I8? Actually G9 is above G8, so not in same row.  \n\nBetter: check for **vertical threat** at G9:  \nColumn G: G7 X, G8 X, G9 X, G10 O — blocks at G10, so only three in column.  \n\nWhat about **diagonal threats**?  \n\nFrom D8 down-right: D8 X, E9 X, F10 ., G11 ., H12 . — no.  \n\nBut consider **C9** (empty):  \nRow 8–9: C8 X, C9 X, D8 X, E8 O — not strong.  \n\nBest might be **creating a new four with two open ends** or an open three.  \n\nActually, look at **F9 and E9**:  \nE9 X, F9 X already. Add D9 (empty):  \nD9 X would give D8 X, E9 X, F9 X — but not aligned in row (D8 is row 8, E9 row 9 — not same row). So not a straight line.  \n\nGiven the board’s