7=O, D8=X, D9=X, D13=X. That’s only 3 X’s.  \n\nActually, look at **column G**: G7=X, G8=X, G9=X, G10=O, G12=O — so three X’s then blocked.  \n\nCheck **column H**: H4=O, H6=O, H8=X, H9=O — only one X.  \n\nBut maybe there’s a **five in a row already**? Let’s check row 8: from A8 to D8: X X X X (four X’s), then E8=O blocks. From F8 to I8: X X X X (four X’s), then J8=O blocks. From K8 to O8: X O X X X — not five.  \n\nSo no immediate five in a row on board yet.\n\nBut — open four?  \n\nLook at **A8 to D8** = X X X X, but E8=O blocks right, left side is board edge (no col before A). So it’s a “dead four” (blocked both sides).  \n\nSimilarly **F8 to I8** = X X X X, left side E8=O, right side J8=O, so dead four.  \n\nSo no direct winning move via simple four.\n\n**Step 4 – Open three threats**  \nFind an open three (three X’s with both ends open) that can become a four in two ways.  \n\nLook at D9, E9, F9, G9 are four in a row already, but both ends blocked (C9=O, H9=O), so not useful.\n\nCheck **diagonal from D13 to H9**: D13=X, E12=O (breaks).  \n\nCheck **diagonal from C8 to G12**: C8=X, D9=X, E10 empty, F11=O — not three.\n\nActually, look at **B8, C8, D8, E8**: B8=X, C8=X, D8=X, E8=O — so three X’s then blocked. Left side A8=X (so actually A8–D8 is four X’s).  \n\nBut maybe **M8, N8, O8**: M8=X, N8=X, O8=X — three in a row at end of row 8, but right side is board edge, left side L8=O blocks? Wait L8=O, so M8–O8 is three X’s but left side L8=O, right side edge, so dead three.  \n\nSo no open three obvious.\n\n**Step 5 – Look for move to create a four with two open ends**  \nConsider **D9–G9** already four X’s blocked both ends.  \n\nWhat about **column I**: I5=O, I8=X, I9=X — only two X’s.  \n\nMaybe check **J column**: J8=O, J10=O — no X’s.  \n\nLet’s think: Black has many X’s scattered but no immediate win. Maybe a move like **F10**?  \n\nCheck: F11=O, F9=X, F8=X — that’s two X’s in column F. Not strong.  \n\nBut notice: **Row 9** has D9–G9 as X’s. If we play E10, then D9–G9 vertical? No, that’s row 9 horizontal. Actually D9–G9 horizontal blocked at C9=O and H9=O.  \n\nWhat about playing **I10**? Then I8, I9, I10 = X X X vertical three, open top I7 empty, bottom I11 empty — that’s an open three! That’s a winning threat because from open three, opponent can’t block both ends next turn.  \n\nLet’s verify: I8=X, I9=X, I10 empty now, I11 empty, I5=O above I8, I7 empty. So yes, I10 creates vertical open three I8–I9–I10 with I7 and I11 empty. White cannot block both in one move, so Black wins next turn.  \n\nAlso check if White has any winning move after I10 — White doesn’t have four anywhere, so safe.\n\n**Step 6 – Confirm I10 is not occupied**  \nOccupied list doesn’t have I10. Yes, G10 and J10 are occupied, I10 is free.\n\nSo **I10** is the winning move (creates an open three that guarantees a win next turn).\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation:** I10 creates a vertical open three (I8, I9, I10) with both ends open (I7 and I11 empty), which is an unstoppable threat.\n\n**Reason:** White cannot block both ends of the open three in one move, so Black will win on the following turn no matter what White plays.\n\n**Answer:** {"col": "I", "row": 10}'}
