y possible overlooked immediate win is **vertical at I-column**: I5(O), I6(empty), I7(O), I8(X), I9(X), I10(X), I11(empty), etc. I8-I9-I10 are three in a row vertically for Black, but need two more.  \n\nLet\'s check **diagonal from H6 to D10**: H6(O), G7(X), F8(X), E8(O) — broken.  \n\nWait — what about **D9, E9, F9, G9, H9**?  \nD9(X), E9(X), F9(X), G9(X), H9(O) — interrupted at H9.  \n\nSo G9 is four in a row horizontally on row 9: D9–E9–F9–G9 are X’s; to the left is C9(O), to the right is H9(O) — blocked both ends, not open four.  \n\nBut look again at **row 9**: C9(O), D9(X), E9(X), F9(X), G9(X), H9(O).  \nIf Black plays **B9** (occupied already — yes, B9=O), no. If Black plays **I9** (occupied X), no.  \n\nActually, the only lethal threat I see for Black:  \n**Column H**: H4(O), H5(empty), H6(O), H7(empty), H8(X), H9(O) — no.  \n\nHmm — maybe there is no immediate 5-in-a-row. Then strategy shifts:  \n\nCheck for **open three that can become open four** and unstoppable.  \n\nFor example, **D8, D9, D13** — need two in between, not close.  \n\nLet’s check **diagonal from D7 to H3**: D7(O), E8(O), F9(X), G10(O) — no.  \n\nBetter: **A8 to E4** diagonal: A8(X), B9(O) — broken.  \n\nGiven the occupied list includes all stones on board, no empty spot in that list.  \nWe must choose an empty spot that creates biggest threat.  \n\nPromising moves:  \n1. **F10** (connects F9, G10, E8 maybe diagonal threat; also vertical F8–F9–F10).  \n2. **H7** (connects H6–H8; vertical and horizontal potential).  \n3. **J9** (connects I8–I9–I10–J10–K11 diagonal threat and J8–J9–J10 vertical).  \n\nMost forcing: **H7** creates vertical three H6(O)–H7(X)–H8(X) (but H6 is O, so not pure Black three) — not ideal.  \n\nActually, **J9** seems strong:  \n- Diagonal from I8(X) to K10(empty) through J9(X) to K11(O) maybe later;  \n- Horizontal from J8(O)–J9(X)–J10(O) — not strong;  \n- But vertical J8(O)–J9(X)–J10(O) — no.  \n\nBetter is **F10**:  \n- Makes vertical F8(X), F9(X), F10(X), F11(O) 