y, maybe **C8–F8** is 4? C8-X, D8-X, E8-O — no.  \n\nWhat about **H8–K8**: H8-X, I8-X, J8-O — no.  \n\nWhat about **I8–L8**: I8-X, J8-O — no.  \n\nSo maybe there is **no immediate winning move**? But the instruction says “If you have any locations to immediately form a 5-in-a-row, you should play that position to win.”  \n\nIf no immediate win, we must choose best strategic move.  \n\nGiven the board, Black has strong center control but is blocked in many lines.  \n\nBest move might be **E6** — creates multiple threats (connects D7 and F8 diagonally, and vertical with E7).  \n\nBut the problem says: “Find a winning move that is legally valid (Is it in the \'Occupied\' list?).”  \n\nSo maybe they expect me to see that **J9** is empty and creates a diagonal 5 with? Let’s check:  \n\nDiagonal \\ from J9 to N13: J9-X (play), K10 empty, L11 empty — no.  \n\nI think I must have missed a direct 5-in-a-row possibility.  \n\nLet’s brute check:  \n\nRow 9: D9–G9 = 4 in a row. If we play at C9 or H9 — both O, so no.  \n\nColumn I: I8–I11 = 4 in a row. If we play at I7 or I12 — both O, so no.  \n\nRow 8: F8–I8 = 4 in a row. If we play at E8 or J8 — both O, so no.  \n\nDiagonal \\: G7–J10: G7-X, H8-X, I9-X, J10-O — so 3 in a row only.  \n\nDiagonal /: D7–A10: D7-X, C8-X, B8-X, A8-X — that’s 4 in a row from A8–D7 diagonal! Yes!  \n\nCheck diagonal / from A8 to D7: A8-X, B8-X, C8-X, D7-X — that’s 4 in a row on diagonal!  \n\nA8 (X), B8 (X), C8 (X), D7 (X) — diagonal up-right:  \n\nCoordinates: A8(1,8), B8(2,8), C8(3,8), D7(4,7) — wait, that’s not a straight diagonal in grid indexing? Let’s map:  \n\nA8 = col A row 8  \nB8 = col B row 8 — same row, not diagonal. So I made a mistake — they are not on same diagonal.  \n\nLet’s check diagonal from A8 to D5: A8, B7, C6, D5 — all empty except A8.  \n\nSo my mistake.  \n\nGiven the complexity, maybe the intended immediate win is at **J9** because:  \n\nRow 9: If we play J9, then I9–M9? I9=X, J9=X, K9 empty — no.  \n\nActually, maybe they